 "postHocFindings (post hoc, not part of the rule)": [
  "post hoc: the ranking is strong where the yes/no threshold was not: on the same fresh-3 data where every yes/no design failed (round 9), all rankers beat keywordNarrow by about 0.18-0.29 MAP, intervals well above 0.",
  "post hoc: the daily-precision table makes the keyword lists look 3x better (0.69 vs 0.25 at N=3), but it is not a like-for-like comparison under the definition fixed before the run: a keyword list shows only the day's matches (often 1, never padded to N) and days with no match are left out (822 empty unit-days for narrow in window, 1,254 in pool), while a ranker shows N articles every day. Most profiles have under one relevant article a day, so a ranker's daily precision is capped by the base rate. The best possible daily precision (oracle: relevant first, same definition) is window 0.274/0.198/0.115 and pool 0.229/0.164/0.095 for N=3/5/10; desc+kw reaches 0.247/0.188/0.113 and 0.210/0.156/0.094, i.e. 90-99% of the cap. A fair product view would be precision with recall per day or per week; not tested.",
  "post hoc: taught answers help the ranking mainly through the keyword evidence and the binormal in the pool protocol (desc 0.684 -> desc+kw 0.766); robotics 0.486 -> 0.696, health 0.511 -> 0.737, courts 0.667 -> 0.865.",
  "post hoc: in the window protocol, profiles with one relevant answer lose from teaching: earnings desc 0.977 -> desc+kw 0.867, courts 0.693 -> 0.514 (relevant sd at its 0.05 floor makes the posterior saturate and the keyword evidence then dominates the order).",
  "post hoc: truncated+kw does not beat desc+kw as a ranker (window 0.698 vs 0.717, pool 0.757 vs 0.766); safety pool 0.446 vs 0.670.",
  "post hoc: fresh-3 is used a second time (round 9 asked the yes/no question), as the rule notes (adaptive reuse); the exam remains the unseen check.",
  "post hoc: the one fallback in a scored unit is truncated+kw robotics window N=5 (Platt), as in round 9; it still ranks (robotics truncated+kw 0.602)."
 ],
